From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Fri, 10 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Let ld set the SAV image's stack address

RT-11's own linker can be told where a program's stack goes: LINK's /M
option takes either the address itself or the name of a global symbol
whose address to use, and writes it into word 042 of the image's header
block.  This gives ld the same option under the name --stack=, for the
one emulation that writes a SAV image.

The default stays RT-11's own, 01000, which is the right answer for a
program whose crt0 asks the monitor for the real top of memory and
moves SP there.  A program that does not do that -- a bare .S with no
crt0, something that has to keep its stack somewhere specific -- had no
way to say so short of patching the header afterwards.

A numeric argument is read the way ld reads numbers everywhere else, so
01000 is octal and 0x200 is the same address in hex; anything that is
not a number is taken as a symbol name.  The symbol is looked up in the
executable this emulation has just linked rather than in the linker's
own hash table, because by the time the SAV conversion runs the link is
over and the output bfd has been closed -- the file on disk is what is
left.  An odd address is refused (the PDP-11 traps on a word push
through an odd SP, and /M refuses one too), as is one outside the 64K
address space, and the resolved address is reported in octal, which is
the one thing the user cannot work out for themselves when the argument
was a symbol.

The address reaches the writer through bfd_sav_pdp11_set_stack_address,
declared in the new bfd/sav-pdp11.h: ld already includes bfd's own
headers this way, and a backend-private header keeps the declaration
next to the definition instead of in generated bfd.h.

Verified: --stack=0100000 puts 0100000 in word 042 and the program
still runs; --stack=_end resolves to the symbol's address; --stack=0x2000
reads as 020000; 01001, nosuchsym and 0x12000 are each refused with the
reason.  Without the option every image is byte-for-byte what it was.
